\section*{Implementation Notes (Phase 4--5)}

\subsection*{Architectural Pivot: ink! Contracts $\rightarrow$ FRAME Pallet}

The original design positioned ink! contracts (RightsManager, SubscriptionHandler, PayPerViewHandler, PurchaseVerifier) as the primary logic layer. During implementation, this was restructured:

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Architectural Pivot --- ink! to FRAME}
{\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{LLL}
\toprule
\textbf{Original} & \textbf{Actual} & \textbf{Rationale} \\
\midrule
\textbf{ink! contracts} as primary logic & \textbf{\texttt{pallet-content-rights}} (FRAME pallet) as primary logic & Direct storage access, native weight system, no contract call overhead \\
\textbf{RightsManager} as orchestrator & Scaffolded but thin API layer & Pallet handles all state; contract provides external interface via pallet-revive precompile \\
\textbf{3 handler contracts} (Subscription, PPV, Purchase) & All logic in single pallet with \texttt{RightsType} enum & Simpler, more efficient, avoids cross-contract calls \\
\textbf{RMRK 2.0} for NFT state & \textbf{\texttt{pallet-nfts}} with attribute-based nesting & RMRK pallets abandoned; pallet-nfts is maintained and compatible \\
\textbf{Chain extensions} for contract$\rightarrow$pallet & \textbf{Custom pallet-revive precompile} at \texttt{0x0000...0400} & Precompiles are the PolkaVM equivalent of chain extensions \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
}
\end{table}

